ife alex as  the prerequisite of a vr headset can be  a hefty expense  but that doesn't detract from the fact  that it is an incredible revisit to the  franchise  and elements you remember while still  being wholly unique to its own design  choices  with the power of valve behind it it's  no surprise that alex released  as one of the most highly polished  experiences that you can see through a  headset  and potentially one that can only truly  be appreciated through that medium  my favorite thing that half-life alex  does is to continue to build  on the world and story that surrounded  the silent gordon freeman for so long  tapping into recognizable characters  while also being something more focused  on its own story  it also brings a new genre of game  within that same universe  playing out more like a survival game  with resources fairly slim and head  crabs being way scarier  when they are on your actual but virtual  face  and we may have had the trip to  ravenholm and half-life 2 to spook us  but many sections straight up feel like  survival horror  but that may also be because i'm a  massive baby  it was a strange sensation but somehow  alex played like the half-life 2 i  remembered  but also obviously different in every  single way i think this game is also the  closest thing we've seen to a system  seller for the virtual reality market  everyone rushing to step back into the  world of the combine invasion  as if g-man himself were muttering the  words right into your ears  if you're once again tempted to visit a  virtual city 17 you may be in luck as  stock's vr headsets  are starting to return if you want to  know exactly what headset to go for  though we have a video we did a week  while back  reviewing the game on every available  device  to me squad sits on the first person  shooter spectrum  somewhere between the battlefield series  and armor which is unsurprising when you  take into account that members of the  development team  worked on battlefields 